    <string name="max_files_help_title">How this screen decides</string>
    <string name="max_files_help_body">There is one extended window with two tabs, and the whole screen belongs to it: no title bar outside it and no second pane. Entries come from one stat pass with a fixed tab-separated format, so ls output is never parsed for data. Anything not measured is shown as unknown rather than as zero, and the status line states how many folders, how many files and how many hidden entries this folder holds, plus the space of its filesystem — reported as “not read” when it could not be measured. Hidden files stay hidden by default and their count stays in that same line, so hiding is announced instead of silent. A long press selects the entry and opens its commands at your finger: copy and cut fill the clipboard, and paste writes into the folder that is open in front of you, asking about name conflicts before anything is written. Every operation is guarded before it reaches the shell: a folder cannot be copied into itself, a move cannot target its own source, and the filesystem root cannot be deleted. Copies, moves, compressions and extractions run as background tasks with a measured percentage — and when a size could not be read, the task says its progress is unknown instead of inventing one. On a screen 600dp or wider the two windows appear side by side, each with its own path and its own count.</string>
